        </div>
        <p>Dominated by European &eacute;migr&eacute; composers who brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. Max Steiner (Austria), Erich Wolfgang Korngold (Austria), Mikl&oacute;s R&oacute;zsa (Hungary), Dimitri Tiomkin (Ukraine), and Franz Waxman (Poland) shaped the sound of epic dramas and sweeping romances. Alfred Newman, the era&rsquo;s most prominent American voice, won twice. Scores were overwhelmingly orchestral, functioning as continuous underscoring that guided audience emotion. Tiomkin and Steiner each won three times; Waxman&rsquo;s consecutive 1950&ndash;1951 wins marked the only back-to-back feat of the era.</p>
