        </div>
        <p>Synthesizers arrived with Vangelis&rsquo; <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981) and Giorgio Moroder&rsquo;s <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978, on the era&rsquo;s cusp). John Williams consolidated his dominance, adding E.T. (1982) and Schindler&rsquo;s List (1993) to his tally. Alan Menken&rsquo;s three-win Disney run (1989, 1991, 1992) was the decade&rsquo;s most concentrated streak. Herbie Hancock&rsquo;s <em>Round Midnight</em> (1986) remains the only jazz-dominated score to win. The era&rsquo;s defining tension: orchestral tradition versus electronic innovation, a battle the Academy mostly resolved in favor of tradition&mdash;but not always.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card contemporary">
        <h3>The Contemporary Era</h3>
        <div class="era-range">~2000 &ndash; 2026</div>
