    <ol class="takeaway-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Composer dominance has decentralized over time.</strong> In the Golden Age, a handful of European &eacute;migr&eacute;s (Steiner, Tiomkin, R&oacute;zsa, Waxman) won repeatedly. By the Contemporary Era, first-time winners account for 69% of ceremonies, and no composer has won more than three times in the 21st century. The Oscar for scoring is no longer a closed circle&mdash;it is an increasingly open field.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The definition of &ldquo;original score&rdquo; has expanded dramatically.</strong> From continuous orchestral underscoring (Steiner) through jazz (Hancock), synthesizers (Vangelis, Moroder), world music (Tan Dun, Rahman), and electronic/ambient textures (Reznor/Ross, Gu&thorn;nad&oacute;ttir), the Academy has progressively broadened what it recognizes. This trend is unlikely to reverse&mdash;future winners will increasingly work across genre boundaries.
      </li>
      <li>
